     "t": "code",
     "lang": "python",
     "code": "\"\"\"\nthreading.Event is a simple synchronization primitive for communication\nbetween threads. An event has an internal boolean flag that is initially\nFalse. Threads can wait for the flag to become True.\n\nMethods:\n  - set()     — set the flag to True; wake all waiting threads.\n  - clear()   — reset the flag to False.\n  - wait(timeout=None) — block until the flag is True or timeout expires.\n                         Returns True if the flag was set, False on timeout.\n  - is_set()  — check the flag state without blocking.\n\nEvents are ideal for:\n  - Signaling between threads (one-shot or recurring).\n  - Thread synchronization barriers.\n  - Graceful shutdown signaling.\n  - Start gates (multiple threads wait, one signal starts them all).\n\nUnlike conditions, events are simpler and don't require holding a lock.\nThey are perfect when you just need a \"go/no-go\" signal.\n\"\"\"\nimport threading\nimport time\n\ndef worker_wait(event, name):\n    print(f\"    {name}: waiting for start signal...\")\n    event.wait()  # Block until event is set\n    print(f\"    {name}: received signal, starting work!\")\n\ndef controller(event):\n    print(\"    Controller: preparing resources...\")\n    time.sleep(0.03)\n    print(\"    Controller: setting event (start signal)!\")\n    event.set()\n\nprint(\"Q282: Events\")\nstart_event = threading.Event()\n\nworkers = [\n    threading.Thread(target=worker_wait, args=(start_event, f\"Worker-{i}\"))\n    for i in range(3)\n]\nfor w in workers:\n    w.start()\n\nctrl = threading.Thread(target=controller, args=(start_event,))\nctrl.start()\n\nfor w in workers:\n    w.join()\nctrl.join()\n\n# Demonstrate timeout\ntimeout_event = threading.Event()\nresult = timeout_event.wait(timeout=0.01)\nprint(f\"  wait(timeout=0.01) returned: {result} (timed out)\")\nprint()",
