     "t": "code",
     "lang": "python",
     "code": "\"\"\"\nSimilar to daemon threads, daemon processes are background processes that\nare automatically terminated when the main process exits. They are set via\nthe daemon property of multiprocessing.Process.\n\n  p = multiprocessing.Process(target=func, daemon=True)\n\nKey behaviors:\n  - Daemon processes are killed when the parent process exits.\n  - They are NOT allowed to create child processes (raises an error).\n  - Useful for fire-and-forget background tasks.\n  - The main process doesn't wait for daemon processes in atexit.\n\nUnlike daemon threads, daemon processes have their own memory space, so\ntermination is cleaner from a memory perspective (the OS reclaims everything).\nHowever, shared resources (files, sockets, shared memory) may still be in\nan inconsistent state.\n\nBest practice: prefer explicit shutdown mechanisms (events, queues with\nsentinel values) over daemon processes for controlled cleanup.\n\"\"\"\nimport multiprocessing\n\nprint(\"Q291: Daemon processes\")\nprint(\"  Daemon process: terminated when parent exits\")\nprint(\"  p = Process(target=func, daemon=True)\")\nprint(\"  Cannot spawn child processes from a daemon process\")\nprint(\"  OS reclaims process memory on termination\")\nprint(\"  Better alternative: use Events or Queue sentinels for shutdown\")\n\n# Demo concept\nclass DaemonProcessDemo:\n    @staticmethod\n    def show_info():\n        p = multiprocessing.Process(target=lambda: None, daemon=True)\n        return f\"daemon={p.daemon}, pid=not-started\"\n\ndemo = DaemonProcessDemo.show_info()\nprint(f\"  Process info: {demo}\")\nprint()",
